# Purpose

Aura Design Language (ADL) defines every visual, interactive and emotional rule of the Aura Platform.

It is the single source of truth for designers, developers and AI-assisted development.

No interface element may be created without following this document.

---

# Design Philosophy

Aura is designed around one belief:

Technology should disappear.

Users should never feel like they are operating machines.

They should feel like they are interacting with their home.

Every screen should communicate calmness, confidence and clarity.

---

# Core Principles

## Calm

The interface never competes for attention.

Information appears only when needed.

Nothing flashes.

Nothing blinks.

Nothing moves without purpose.

---

## Clear

Every screen answers one question.

"What can I do here?"

If the answer is not immediately obvious, the design has failed.

---

## Consistent

Spacing.

Typography.

Animation.

Colors.

Corners.

Everything follows predictable rules.

Consistency builds confidence.

---

## Adaptive

Aura adapts to:

Current room

Connected devices

Time of day

Current activity

Lighting conditions

Device availability

Without requiring user configuration.

---

## Human

Aura never feels mechanical.

Animations are smooth.

Language is natural.

Controls feel physical.

Transitions feel intentional.

---

# Visual Hierarchy

Every screen follows this order.

1 Primary Focus

The most important action.

Large.

Clear.

Immediate.

---

2 Supporting Information

Status.

Context.

Secondary controls.

---

3 Details

Technical information.

Diagnostics.

Advanced settings.

Never compete with the primary content.

---

# Layout System

Aura uses a strict grid.

Large margins.

Large spacing.

Minimal visual noise.

Everything aligns.

Nothing floats randomly.

Whitespace is intentional.

---

# Navigation Philosophy

Maximum depth:

Three levels.

Example

Home

↓

Living Room

↓

Television

Never deeper.

If additional depth is required, redesign the feature.

---

# Screen Structure

Every screen follows the same structure.

Navigation Bar

↓

Hero Content

↓

Primary Controls

↓

Secondary Controls

↓

Additional Information

↓

Context Actions

This hierarchy never changes.

---

# Cards

Cards are the primary interface element.

Every card has one responsibility.

A card should never attempt to solve multiple unrelated problems.

Cards may expand but never become confusing.

---

Card Anatomy

Header

Status

Primary Content

Primary Action

Optional Secondary Action

Context Menu

---

# Floating Elements

Floating controls should be rare.

Allowed:

Floating Scene Button

Floating Search

Floating Diagnostics

Avoid floating menus.

---

# Buttons

Primary

Filled

Largest emphasis

One per screen whenever possible.

---

Secondary

Outlined

Less visual weight.

---

Tertiary

Text only.

---

Danger

Red.

Only for destructive actions.

---

Never place two Primary buttons next to each other.

---

# Icons

Use Apple's SF Symbols whenever possible.

Icons should always support text.

Never rely on icons alone.

Size consistency is mandatory.

---

# Color Usage

Color communicates meaning.

Never decoration.

Blue

Interactive

Green

Success

Orange

Attention

Red

Problem

Gray

Inactive

No other semantic colors should exist.

---

# Typography

Hierarchy

Large Title

Screen Titles

Title

Sections

Headline

Cards

Body

Descriptions

Caption

Metadata

Only these styles are permitted.

---

# Information Density

Aura prefers showing less.

Additional details appear only when requested.

Examples

Tap

Expand

Long Press

Context Menu

---

# Interaction Rules

Tap

Primary interaction.

Double Tap

Never required.

Long Press

Reveal advanced actions.

Swipe

Navigation or card actions.

Pinch

Reserved for future interfaces.

---

# Gestures

Never invent custom gestures.

Follow iOS conventions.

Users should never need instructions.

---

# Feedback

Every action receives feedback.

Visual

Animation

Haptic

State Change

Errors include explanation.

Not codes.

---

# States

Every interactive component supports:

Default

Focused

Pressed

Loading

Disabled

Success

Warning

Error

Offline

Unknown

---

# Loading

Avoid spinners.

Prefer:

Skeleton placeholders.

Progressive loading.

Animated placeholders.

Instant transitions.

---

# Empty States

Empty screens should educate.

Never simply display:

"No Data"

Instead

"No scenes yet.

Create your first scene to automate your home."

---

# Errors

Errors should solve problems.

Every error includes:

What happened

Why

How to fix it

Retry

---

# Motion

Motion explains.

Never decorates.

Animation should communicate:

Movement

Hierarchy

Focus

Completion

State Change

---

# Haptics

Every important action receives tactile confirmation.

Minor actions do not.

The home should feel physical.

---

# Sound

Optional.

Minimal.

Soft.

Never distracting.

---

# Shadows

Shadows communicate depth.

Never realism.

Soft.

Wide.

Subtle.

---

# Blur

Blur separates layers.

Never hides information.

Glass should remain readable.

---

# Corners

Aura uses one corner radius system.

No arbitrary radii.

Consistency is required.

---

# Accessibility

Every feature must support:

VoiceOver

Dynamic Type

Reduce Motion

High Contrast

Large Touch Targets

One-handed operation

Accessibility is a design requirement.

Not an enhancement.

---

# Responsiveness

The interface should always feel alive.

Interactions begin immediately.

Animations never block interaction.

Content updates progressively.

---

# Design Checklist

Before approving any screen ask:

Does it feel calm?

Can a first-time user understand it?

Is there unnecessary information?

Can one element be removed?

Does every component have a purpose?

Does it follow iOS conventions?

Would this still look modern in five years?

If any answer is "No"

The screen is not finished.

---

# Golden Rule

When in doubt...

Remove.

Simplify.

Refine.

Never add complexity unless it creates measurable value.

---

# ADL Motto

Design less.

Experience more.

